  [Escalation] 5 remediation options:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Deploy Backup Operator + Stagger Cell Activity Windows: Bring a certified backup operator (B1, multi-task certified) onto the floor to absorb H1 and H3 workload. Stagger Cell-1 and Cell-2 high-noise operations by 15-minute offset windows so peak noise periods do not overlap, reducing combined simultaneous noise below 82 dB. H2 shifts to lower-intensity tasks in Cell-2. This approach targets all four violations directly without dropping demand or relaxing any safety threshold.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~2.0%
    - Option 2: Cross-Cell Variant Redistribution via AGV Re-routing: Shift the highest cycle-time and noise-generating product variant (e.g., Variant-C) from Cell-1 to Cell-2 during off-peak AGV windows, and reassign the quieter Variant-A runs to Cell-1. Rebalance H1 tasks to Cell-2 where activity density is lower, reducing H1 and H3 fatigue indexes below 0.40. Cell stagger reduces combined noise. AGV schedule must be re-optimized to avoid inter-cell throughput bottlenecks.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~8.0%
    - Option 3: Product Mix Reduction — Defer Variant-C This Shift: Remove the most resource-intensive variant (Variant-C) from the current shift plan entirely. This is the single largest contributor to operator fatigue and noise load. Deferring it eliminates approximately 30% of the excess workload, bringing H1, H2, and H3 fatigue indexes within safe limits and lowering combined noise below 82 dB without any constraint relaxation. Variant-C units are rescheduled to the next available shift or produced via overtime on a separate staffing plan.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~20.0%
    - Option 4: Throughput Reduction to Safe Operating Point (~83% of Demand Target): Scale back the production rate for all variants uniformly to a level at which all four constraints are satisfied simultaneously. Optimization modeling indicates the constraint-feasible frontier sits at approximately 83% of the elevated demand target (i.e., +3.6% over baseline rather than +20%). This incurs a known deadline gap of ~17% for this shift's demand target but requires no operator reassignment, no constraint relaxation, and no product mix change. Gap is communicated to the production manager for customer delivery re-negotiation.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~17.0%
    - Option 5 (Last Resort): Temporary Fatigue Limit Relaxation with Medical Authorization: With explicit sign-off from the occupational health officer and production manager, temporarily raise fatigue index thresholds to H1 ≤ 0.55, H2 ≤ 0.50, and H3 ≤ 0.55 for this shift only, contingent on mandatory 30-minute rest breaks every 90 minutes and continuous biometric monitoring. Noise limit relaxation is NOT recommended and is excluded from this option; a noise engineering control (temporary sound barriers or reduced simultaneous cell activity) must still reduce factory noise to ≤ 82 dB. This option should only be exercised if Options 1–4 are operationally infeasible within the shift window. All deviations must be logged in the compliance record.
